<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Albrecht Dürer</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1471–1528</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Albrecht Dürer was a painter, printmaker and theorist from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a>, the leading artist of the German <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>. His woodcuts and engravings, such as the Apocalypse series, Melencolia I and the Rhinoceros, spread his fame across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and his journeys to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> brought Renaissance ideas north. He also wrote treatises on geometry and human proportion and became a supporter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/albrecht-durer.jpg" alt="Albrecht Dürer" width="320" height="451" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Albrecht Dürer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:D%C3%BCrer_Alte_Pinakothek.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
